t levels of automated driving from level 0 to level 2, drivers are required to drive with their full attention. However, in level 3 conditional automated driving, drivers are permitted to fully engage in non-driving related task (NDRT) unless they are prompted to take control of the vehicle. Studies on NDRT have been extensively conducted (Hungund & Kumar Pradhan, 2023; Jaussein et al., 2021). NDRTs used in conditional automated driving research can be categorised into standardised tasks and naturalistic tasks. While naturalistic tasks resemble real driving scenarios, standardised tasks are cognitive tasks that may be adapted from psychological tests that have been widely used, such as the nback task (Kane et al., 2007; Melnicuk et al., 2021). For standardised cognitive tasks, task and driver engagement levels can be aligned to a range by comparing effects of different cognitive tasks on driver performance. For instance, researchers may increase the difficulty of a cognitive task to enhance driver engagement, thereby facilitating the investigation of corresponding changes in driver performance (Choi et al., 2020; Lu et al., 2021). Technological advances in motor vehicles are requiring a greater level of interaction with the vehicle’s controls and displays, which frequently necessitates the driver to temporarily direct their attention away from the road. Mobile communication systems in vehicles can also distract the driver, and the advent of level 3 automated driving introduces an entirely new set of activities to distract the driver from attending to the road and traffic conditions. The interrelated concepts of driver attention, switching of attention and cognitive load can adversely affect driving performance and road safety, and technological progress is steadily increasing their impact. The purpose of the present review is to provide automotive engineers and designers with a resource with which to understand the cognitive factors that affect driver attention, as well as a review of the related literature for researchers who are interested in investigating distracted driving. It is hoped that this knowledge will lead to the development of safer and more user-friendly driving systems. This review will address the following topics:
• Cognitive psychology concepts relevant to driving task switching: An overview of psychological frameworks related to attention and task switching. • Review method: A review of recent publications in the psychological, ergonomics, and engineering fields concerning task switching and driver distraction. • Cognitive load in driving: A discussion of methods for assessing cognitive load in both manual and conditional automated driving scenarios and the effect of cognitive load on manual and conditional automated driving. • Task switching in driving: The application of task switching theory in driving and the main factors affecting the effectiveness of task transition. • Discussion: We raise road safety concerns that are associated with driver task switching and offer suggestions for mitigating risks.
2. Cognitive psychology concepts relevant to driving task switching
This section will show how concepts developed by psychologists can provide an understanding of the cognitive processes that are brought into play when a driver switches their attention from one task to another, and the implications that these processes have for road safety, particularly in the context of semi-autonomous vehicles. Cognitive psychology encompasses a massive literature, characterised by competing concepts and terminologies, and sometimes conflicting findings. Since the current review is intended for an audience of non-psychologists, psychological concepts and terminologies are presented here in their simplest forms without a discussion of the nuances.
2.1. Cognitive process during driving
The psychological basis of cognitive changes while driving has its basis in the concept of cognitive load. This concept assumes that cognitive architecture includes long-term memory (LTM) and working memory (WM) (Sweller, 2011; Sweller & Sweller, 2006). LTM involves the retrieval into consciousness of previous experiences and can be strengthened through learning, practice, and repetition, often on a subconscious level (Skaugset et al., 2016). For the purpose of this review, WM can be considered to be synonymous with short-term memory and it can be conceptualized as a kind of workstation with limited capacity in which new and old information is
