constantly being transformed, combined, and updated (Baddeley, 1992; Chase & Ericsson, 1982; Solso et al., 2005). Most people can retain up to 5–7 separate bits (or ’chunks’) of information in WM, and if the requirement to store new information surpasses this capacity, some bits of information are erased, in a situation known as information overload. Cognitive load refers to the amount of information that WM is required to store and operate on at a given time. As the number of bits of information increases, the effort required to retain these bits in WM becomes greater (Atkinson & Shiffrin, 1968). Some researchers use the term “cognitive workload” rather than “cognitive load” (Cooper et al., 2013; Patten et al., 2006; von Janczewski et al., 2021). Cognitive workload is a similar term with a subtle difference: cognitive load is correlated with task difficulty whereas cognitive workload takes into account individual differences in ability (McKendrick & Harwood, 2019); the same cognitive load can result in different workload levels, as individuals have different maximum WM capacities. The present review refers only to cognitive load, as individual differences are not a focus of discussion. Driver behaviour studies might the use term cognitive load to refer the mental demand imposed on the driver by a nondriving task, especially non-visual tasks (Goodridge et al., 2023). In the present paper, cognitive load includes both the driving task and the distracting task. Undertaking a cognitive task requires attention. In cognitive psychology, attention is defined as “the concentration of mental effort on specific sensory or mental events” (Schneider & Shiffrin, 1977; Solso et al., 2005). In attention and cognitive control, human performance is hypothesised to be driven by two modes, automatic and controlled (Servant et al., 2018). Automaticity allows people to perform tasks fast, efficiently, unconsciously, and goal-unrelatedly (Servant et al., 2018) and involves little cognitive load. Automaticity theories posit that through repeated practice, the control of cognitive processes shifts from a reliance on WM to becoming automatic and guided by the retrieval processes of LTM. The main limitation of the automatic/involuntary process is inflexibility, which means the response can be inappropriate when conditions change (Eysenck & Keane, 2020). In contrast to automatic processes, non-automatic/voluntary processes require attention and engagement of WM (Shiffrin & Schneider, 1977), which require a cognitive load, though they can be used flexibly in changing circumstances (Eysenck & Keane, 2020). Classically, the control of attention has been categorised into two paths: top-down attention (goal-directed) and bottom-up (stimulusdriven) (Anderson et al., 2021). When people are undertaking a task, such as driving, they must be able to maintain concentration on the task and resist interference from irrelevant information. ’Top-down’ attention refers to search strategies that are consciously implemented by the individual (e.g. looking out for stop signs), while ’bottom-up’ attention involves the perception of salient stimuli that involuntarily capture our attention (e.g. noticing flashing lights on a police car). While both forms of attention are important for driving, many bottom-up stimuli are irrelevant and can distract the driver. Attention is considered to be related to WM capacity (Kane & Engle, 2002; Oberauer, 2019). It has been proposed that people with a high WM capacity are generally better at maintaining topdown attentional control and inhibiting responses to bottom-up stimuli (Furley & Memmert, 2012; Hiebel & Zimmer, 2015). Recently a third path was recognised. Selection history, which involves learning and practice and is stored in LTM, has been shown to influence attention and responses to familiar situations (Anderson et al., 2021). Fig. 1 summarises the involvement of attention and cognitive processing during driving. Cognitive proc