appropriate action as necessary until the destination is reached. The retention of sensory input (e.g. a police car ahead) in working memory is influenced by both bottom-up (e.g. noticing flashing lights) and top-down (e.g. maintaining the car within the lane) attentional processes and is modified by goals, tasks, expectations, and recent history. Information accumulates on more active goals until the driver has assessed the situation and selected a response. Executive control coordinates tasks and goals, including suspending and switching from one task to another. Situation assessment and action planning are also under executive control. Automatic responses stored in LTM assist with situation assessment and action planning that guide the execution of actions. An important aspect of top-down attention is the extent to which available cognitive processes or energy are allocated to the task. As a person turns their attention to a task, the amount of attention given to that task can vary from a cursory involvement to an exclusive involvement. Someone who is fully focussed on a task does so to the exclusion of all other tasks and inhibits or blocks incoming stimuli that would normally capture their attention. Under this condition, the phenomenon of people failing to notice a salient and fully visible, but unexpected object or event is known as inattentional blindness. (Kreitz, Furley, Memmert, & Simons, 2015). For example, when a novice driver fully engrossed in driving on a road with complex road conditions is asked a question, they might not respond because they were not consciously aware that a question had been asked. This capacity to concentrate deeply on a task, to the exclusion of other tasks and stimuli, has been variously termed focussed attention, sustained attention, attentional stability, and task focus. Focussed attention is influenced by factors such as motivation, drive, and arousal; it can be decreased by the passage of time and by external distractors, furthermore, individuals differ in their capacity for focused attention (Cohen, 2014). Focussed attention is counterbalanced by cognitive flexibility, which refers to the capacity to readily switch attention from one task to another in response to internal or external cues. If focused attention is not allocated, it may cause change blindness, a phenomenon where people fail to observe visual changes that are easily seen once noticed (Rensink, 2009). For example, drivers may fail to notice a road sign or a pedestrian, which happens more in urban scenes than in rural scenes (Beanland et al., 2017).
2.2. Task switching
The previous section introduced basic psychological concepts and terminology involved in driving, especially cognitive load and attention. Building on this, driving with a distracting task brings the challenge of multitasking. Multitasking, particularly in the context of driving, presents a significant challenge to performance and safety. Drivers who engage in secondary tasks while driving are effectively multitasking, which involves either performing tasks concurrently or alternating between them. Multitasking can be broadly categorized into two forms: concurrent multitasking, where tasks are performed simultaneously, and serial multitasking, which involves task-switching (MacPherson, 2018). The impact of multitasking on task performance has been extensively studied, particularly with regard to the allocation of cognitive resources. To elucidate the mechanisms underlying the observed performance decrements during multitasking, the Multiple Resource Theory (MRT) offers a framework for understanding how different tasks compete for limited cognitive resources. Wickens proposed a four-dimensional computational model that categorizes mental resources along the following dimensions: processing stages (cognitive vs. motor tasks), processing codes (spatial vs. verbal tasks), modalities (auditory vs. visual tasks), and visual channels (focal vs. ambient vision) (Wickens, 2008; Wickens, 2014). This multidimensional model clarifies why tasks that draw on the same mental resources are more likely to interfere with one another, thereby reducing overall performance. In the context of driving, the MRT model is particularly useful for understanding how the cognitive demands of operating a vehicle interact with the demands of engaging in secondary tasks, potentially compromising driver performance and safety. As automation levels increase, drivers’ multitasking is more likely to involve task switching, as they have greater opportunities to engage in non-driving tasks compared to lower levels of automation (e.g., Level 0 or Level 1). When drivers shift their attention between driving and these secondary tasks, this task switching can impact their attention and performance. Task switching frequently occurs in everyday life and is considered to be at the nexus of executive control in the human cognitive system (Schneider & Logan, 2009). Task switching prolongs reaction time (RT) compared to non-switching trials (Monsell, 2003). This is because when people switch attention between tasks, each cognitive task requires an appropriate configuration of mental resources, which is a task set, and it takes time for people to initiate a different task set (Monsell, 2003). There are two main types of switch cost, local switch cost and global switch cost. An example of a task switching paradigm is shown in Fig. 2. This paradigm is composed of task switching trials and task repetition trials. The reaction delay and performance deterioration caused by switching between tasks is called local switch cost (Schneider & Logan, 2009). Combined with the paradigm, the performance (in terms of RT and error rate) of trial 4 (switched from trial 3) is usually better than trial 3 (switched from trial 2), and the improved performance is attributed to the lack of a local switch cost (Strobach et al., 2012). Additionally, in a paradigm composed entirely consecutive trials of Task A (or Task B), the responses are usually quicker and more accurate than when the same task is repeated in the task switching paradigm. In this situation, the
