3. Review method
The primary focus of this review is to elucidate the cognitive processes that occur when a driver in a level 3 conditional automated vehicle is required to shift their attention from engaging in NDRTs to taking control of the moving vehicle. Since similar cognitive processes occur during distracted driving in level 0–2 automated vehicles, distracted manual driving is also reviewed. This comprehensive narrative review delves into the relationship between driver cognitive load and cognitive tasks as secondary activities during manual driving and conditional automated driving (ranging from level 0 to level 3 automation). To compile relevant data, an extensive search was conducted through electronic databases, including Google Scholar, Scopus, PubMed, and ScienceDirect. Relevant keywords regarding cognitive load and task switching during driving were applied, including effect / influence of cognitive task / standardised task / driving distraction / multitasking / task switching / on workload / cognitive function / mental workload / cognitive load during driving / automated driving / driving. The selected data sources encompassed a variety of scholarly outputs, including peer-reviewed manuscripts, conference proceedings, book chapters, books, reports, and web pages, all available in English. While the review did not impose a restriction on the publication year, preference was given to studies published within the last 5 years to ensure a contemporary perspective on the subject. This review investigates the relationship between cognitive tasks and driving task performance. Since it is technically challenging to quantify the effect of naturalistic tasks on driving performance precisely, most studies in this area have used standardised cognitive tasks instead. For standardised cognitive tasks, task and driver engagement levels can be aligned to a range by comparing the effects of different cognitive tasks. While most studies included in this review used standardised cognitive tasks, studies that used naturalistic tasks such as interactions with cell phones, were identified, compared, and discussed, enriching the overall analysis. Papers were excluded from consideration by the present review if: (a) driving performance and cognitive task performance were not measured or shown; (b) the cognitive task was not a quantifiable variable or the cognitive task was not applied in direct association with the driving task; (c) the paper presented data that had previously been published by the same author; (d) the paper focused on participants with pre-existing impairments of cognitive function.
4. Cognitive load in driving
