4.1. Types of driving tasks and cognitive tasks
Driving in a simulator is the most common and safest method for evaluating driving performance in response to task switching and changes in cognitive load (Blane, Falkmer, Lee, & Dukic Willstrand, 2018; Devlin, McGillivray, Charlton, Lowndes, & Etienne, 2012; Lew, Rosen, Thomander, & Poole, 2009; Vance, Fazeli, Ball, Slater, & Ross, 2014; Anderson, Rizzo, Shi, Uc, & Dawson, 2005). When investigating cognitive load, the driver is required to perform secondary tasks, which can be naturalistic or standardised. While naturalistic tasks resemble real-world situations, allowing for studying issues that occur in everyday life (Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2016), it is difficult to quantify cognitive load and participant engagement levels. Consequently, standardised cognitive tests of WM are preferred as they provide objective and quantifiable outcomes (von Janczewski et al., 2021). The International Organization for Standardization (ISO/TS 14198:2019) has recommended that the Critical Tracking task, Surrogate Reference task (SuRT) and n-back task be used as calibration tasks (secondary task) for the assessment of driver demand due to the use of in-vehicle systems. It also recommended the Lane Change Test (LCT) and Detection-Response Task (DRT) be used as the primary tasks. Other primary tasks include navigating, path following, manoeuvring, avoiding obstacles, and controlling speed (ISO, 2019). LCT, as one of the primary tasks, is conducted on a real or simulated three-lane straight road with a series of lane changes defined by signs. In this test, a participant is instructed to follow the signs that appear at approximately regular intervals on both sides of the track to drive in the designated lanes. ISO (ISO/TS 26022:2010) recommends using the LCT in a dual-task scenario to measure human performance degradation on a primary driving-like task while a secondary task is being performed (ISO, 2010). Cognitive tasks that are commonly used in driving research are shown in Fig. 4. In the Critical Tracking Task, the driver is required to prevent a horizontal bar presented on a computer screen from deviating from a predetermined position (Petzoldt et al., 2014) as shown in Fig. 4a, however, this task is infrequently used even though it is a standard task. In the SuRT, drivers are shown several circles of equal size alongside one larger target circle and are required to identify the target circle (Fig. 4b). The task difficulty varies based on the size difference between the target circle and the others, ranging from easy to challenging demand levels. The n-back task is a recall task that can be presented visually or audibly, where participants must indicate whether the same stimulus was presented to them on a previous occasion (Fig. 4c). The difficulty of this task can be manipulated by changing the value of ’n’. For instance, 1-back refers to the preceding presentation, while 3-back refers to the stimulus that was presented three presentations earlier. A review of the effect of nback tasks on cognitive load in manual driving found a medium-to-large effect size on degraded driving performance (von Janczewski et al., 2021). While this confirms the n-back task as a useful tool for studying driver cognitive load, the present authors prioritised papers using the n-back task published since 2021, to avoid duplicating the content of Janczewski’s review. Additional psychological cognitive tasks have been used in driving research. The Arrow task requires participants to select an arrow in a 4 × 4 or 6 × 6 matrix to match the target (Hamish Jamson & Merat, 2005). In the Simon task, participants are required to respond to two different stimuli (from left or right) using two different buttons (left or right). They respond faster and more accurately if both the stimulus and response features are congruent (when the stimulus and the response are on the same side), and slower and less accurately when they are incongruent (when the stimulus is on the other side to the response) (Boudreau & Strybel, 2022). Requiring a driver to perform a secondary task imposes additional cognitive and attentional demands. The effect of increased cognitive load on driving quality is assessed by comparing driving performance to a control condition where the individual is required to focus on driving in the absence of any secondary tasks. The metrics used to assess driving quality include steering wheel-based parameters such as the standard deviation of the steering angle, lateral position-based parameters such as the time spent out of the lane and the standard deviation of lateral position (SDLP) (Xu et al., 2024), and velocity-related parameters such as the standard deviation of longitudinal velocity. Similarly, the extent of cognitive load can be assessed by comparing performance on the secondary tasks (e.g. error rate, reaction time (RT), and response rate) in the absence of the driving task. In the DRT, the driver is required to press a button when they detect a simple stimulus, which can be visual, tactile or auditory, and then their RT and response rate are recorded. Technically the DRT is a secondary task, as it is additional to the task of driving, and imposes a cognitive load; nonetheless, the load is considered to be minimal and it is generally used in combination with other secondary tasks to assess the impact of those tasks on cognitive load (Stojmenova et al., 2017; Stojmenova & Sodnik, 2018; van Winsum, 2018). For this reason, the DRT is often regarded as a primary task. In summary, driving performance researchers use DRT and other driving-related tasks as the primary tasks, and cognitive tasks that are not related to driving to elevate drivers’ cognitive load as the secondary task or NDRTs.
4.2. Cognitive load in manual driving
From SAE automation level 0 to level 2, driving is the primary task for drivers. This is referred to as manual driving, regardless of whether a manual or automatic transmission is involved. Drivers must monitor their environment and maintain safe driving, which demands ongoing cognitive involvement. Numerous studies have examined the deleterious effects of factors such as old age (Depestele et al., 2020), disease (Dobbs et al., 2005; Ranchet et al., 2012; Shimonaga et al., 2021), alcohol intoxication (Garrisson et al., 2021), and medications (Giorgetti et al., 2015). For drivers with normal cognitive function, multitasking has a significant impact on road safety. This is because engaging in additional tasks divides the driver’s attention and elevates their cognitive load, which in turn, increases the risk of errors (Regan et al., 2011). The slower reaction times and disrupted performance lead to a higher rate of accidents (Overton et al., 2015). While driving distraction can also occur in low cognitive load situations (e.g. mind wandering) (Martens & Brouwer, 2013), the present paper focusses on the high cognitive load imposed by secondary tasks. Table 1 summarises 13 papers that evaluated driver cognitive load using different cognitive tasks. These papers primarily concern
