driver distraction, multitasking and Advanced Driver-Assistance Systems (ADAS, applied in level 1 and level 2). Most cognitive tasks were presented audibly, visually, or in combination. Tasks such as the auditory version of the n-back task increased the driver’s cognitive load without visual distraction. Studies using both visual and auditory tasks have found that visual distraction has a larger effect on driving performance than auditory distraction. For example, the auditory n-back task did not impair lateral out-of-lane performance whereas the visual n-back task led to significant impairment (Adachi et al., 2023). Similarly, using different types of visual and auditory cognitive tasks, it was found that the visual arrow task and SuRT decreased driving stability to a greater extent than the auditory n-back task (Ma et al., 2024; Yang, Ye, Easa, Feng, & Zheng, 2023). This can be explained using Multiple Resource Theory, which suggests that tasks relying on the same mental resources are more likely to interfere with each other (Wickens, 2008). Since driving relies heavily on visual processing, visual tasks like the arrow task and SuRT are more likely to disrupt driving performance compared to auditory tasks. This finding is further supported by research using naturalistic tasks. Wechsler and colleagues, for example, investigated driver multitasking with tasks such as typing, reasoning, and memory, and found that visual tasks had a more negative impact on driving than auditory tasks (Wechsler et al., 2018). Compared to other distraction sources, mobile phone use is becoming increasingly a concern. Researchers reviewed the impacts of mobile phone distraction on driving performance, finding that texting while driving, which requires both manual and visual attention, can cause visual, manual, cognitive or even auditory distraction, leading to serious traffic incidents (Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2016; Voinea et al., 2023). In Table 1, some papers were primarily interested in driving performance and used cognitive tasks to increase drivers’ cognitive load, while others used performance on cognitive tasks to evaluate the driver’s cognitive load. Most papers found that driving performance was impaired when cognitive load was increased by having to perform secondary tasks. For instance, when the DRT was applied as an additional cognitive load, drivers’ SDLP increased, indicating that the extra cognitive load reduced driving stability
(  ̈Oztürk et al., 2023). Furthermore, the SuRT caused drivers to spend more time out of the lane centre in both level 0 and level 2 manual driving, and conversely, drivers had a higher SuRT score when the cognitive load of the driving tasks was lower (Yang et al., 2023). In manual driving, if a secondary task is introduced, the driver must divide their attention between the driving task and the secondary task, leading to a decline in performance on both tasks. When a 2-back task and DRT were applied in a car-following scenario, it was found that compared to a control condition where there was no extra cognitive task, driving stability decreased in terms of car position control and speed control (Zhai et al., 2020). When the 1-back and 2-back paradigms were compared, it was found that DRT performance and driving performance decreased more significantly in the 2-back condition, indicating that higher levels of cognitive
load have a progressively negative impact on driving performance (  ̈Oztürk et al., 2023). To summarise the main findings of Table 1, secondary tasks, especially visual ones, impair driving performance in manual driving. The degree of impairment increases as the task load increases, and performance on secondary tasks also suffers as attention is divided between the secondary task and driving. It is notable that most of these studies had relatively small numbers of participants (mostly 20–25) and the mean age of the participants was generally less than 30 years, so the generality of these findings to larger and more diverse populations remains to be determined.
4.3. Cognitive load in conditional automated driving
4.3.1. Measurements of driver performance
The primary parameters used to measure driver performance are vehicle-based metrics. In research involving level 3 conditional automated driving, participants are generally required to engage in NDRTs while the vehicle is under automatic control, to ascertain their effect on the speed and accuracy of the takeover response. Once the takeover request is launched, drivers are requested to resume manual driving. Vehicle-based driving performance enables objective comparisons of manual driving ability in the absence of an intervention (i.e. baseline manual driving) and following an intervention (e.g. after a takeover request). In addition to driving performance, many studies have examined cognitive load following a takeover request. Table 2 summarises 16 recent papers on conditional automated driving that have used cognitive tasks. Eight of those papers assessed cognitive task performance with standardised tasks (Choi et al., 2020; Du et al., 2020; Melnicuk, Thompson, Jennings, & Birrell, 2021; Meteier et al., 2022; Radlmayr, Gold, Lorenz, Farid, & Bengler, 2014; Ucin ́ska, Odachowska, Gąsiorek, & Kruszewski, 2021) and other tasks (Peng et al., 2023; Ucin ́ska et al., 2021). The other 8 studies applied standardised cognitive tasks to differentiate drivers’ cognitive load levels but did not evaluate performance on the cognitive task. The SuRT was used both as a visual NDRT in conditional automated driving and as a standard task to control participants’ visual attention and cognitive engagement. Unlike studies with manual driving where nback tasks were mostly presented aurally, in conditional automated driving, the n-back tasks were presented both aurally and visually. Researchers choosing multiple types of cognitive tasks tend to differentiate cognitive demand types and observe their effect on driver takeover behaviours. Driver cognitive load variation during the takeover transition can also be followed by physiological parameters, such as the electrocardiogram (Meteier et al., 2022), heart rate variability (Du, Yang, & Zhou, 2020; Meteier et al., 2022; Chu, Wang, Deng, Lu, & Wu, 2020), galvanic skin responses (Du et al., 2020), respiration rate (Meteier et al., 2022) and electroencephalogram (Yang et al., 2023). Most papers recorded takeover driving performance using hands-on RT, lateral position change and steering behaviour. Eye-tracking is used to assess changes in the speed and direction of driver gaze (Du, Kim, et al., 2020; Feldhütter et al., 2017; Petermeijer et al., 2017). The most common method for subjective evaluation or self-report questionnaires is the NASA task load index. Other methods include the driving activity load index (Lu et al., 2021), visual analogue scale (Choi et al., 2020), and other questionnaires. In summary, driver performance measurement includes vehicle-based driving performance, cognitive task performance, eyetracking and gaze, physiological parameters and subjective questionnaires.
