4.3.2. Effects of cognitive tasks
The impact of cognitive tasks on driver takeover performance was detailed in Table 2, outlining two distinct categories. The first category examines the effect of cognitive load during automated driving on driver takeover performance. The second category examines how different types or levels of cognitive tasks during automated driving affect driver takeover performance. In the first category, nine papers compared the effect of cognitive tasks in conditional automated driving. Eight of these papers found that engaging in cognitive tasks during automated driving impaired driving performance, as indicated by slower takeover RT or response time, decreased driving stability, increased subjective demand evaluation, and changed physiological parameters. However, one study found that the takeover RT was longer when there was no cognitive task, although the difference was not statistically significant (Lu et al., 2021). This exception may be due to cognitive underload. Automation reduces operators’ mental workload, and mental underload can be as detrimental as overload, because attentional capacity shrinks to accommodate reductions in mental workload, and the shrinkage is responsible for the performance decrement (McWilliams & Ward, 2021; Young & Stanton, 2002a, 2002b). Lu and colleagues discovered that under high driving task loads (such as manual driving and level 2 driving), drivers exhibited deteriorating performance (such as lane change or crash) with increasing cognitive task complexity. Conversely, under lower driving task loads (in level 3 assisted driving), driving performance showed less susceptibility to the cognitive task. These findings align with the idea that driving and cognitive task loads combine additively, leading to poorer driving performance. In the second category, five studies investigated the impact of various levels of n-back tasks. Four of these studies concluded that as the difficulty of the n-back task increased, driving performance declined in terms of reaction time (RT) and driving stability. Elevated heart rate and changes in respiration rate correlate with driving performance suggesting that heightened cognitive load affects drivers’ physiological performance (Melnicuk et al., 2021). In addition, it was observed that the n-back performance decreased when the difficulty of the n-back task increased (Melnicuk et al., 2021; Meteier et al., 2022). The five papers also compared different types of cognitive tasks. While differences in driver performance were observed with different types of cognitive tasks, no consistent finding
