5. Task switching in driving
In task switching experiments, a task typically involves performing a mental operation or action in response to a stimulus (Kiesel et al., 2010). Task switching refers to the flexible transition from one task to another (Parasuraman & Caggiano, 2002), where attention shifts from one task to another either voluntarily or involuntarily (Mochi & Madjar, 2018). In the context of driving, task switching refers to a driver shifting their attention between driving and a distracting task, or vice versa. Task switching during driving is common in daily life, such as reading a message on a phone or using an in-vehicle navigation function while driving. In conditional automated driving, drivers can focus on NDRTs and switch back to driving when requested. Since both conditions are similar to task switching scenarios used in psychological research (Shi & Bengler, 2022b), task switching theories can help us to understand why secondary tasks impair driving performance (Hecht et al., 2020; Shi & Bengler, 2022a).
5.1. Similarity of cognitive task switching and driving task switching
Task switching during driving is illustrated diagrammatically in Fig. 5 and Fig. 6. Fig. 5 relates to manual driving where the driver is required to simultaneously perform a secondary task, whereas Fig. 6 relates to automated driving where the driver is required to switch from an NDRT to taking over manual control of the vehicle. In manual driving, driving while performing a secondary task is a form of multitasking. Multitasking research includes research on dual-tasking performance, and task switching performance (Pashler, 2000; Strobach et al., 2018). When a secondary task is introduced (Fig. 5), attention may be distributed in one of two ways: either both tasks are performed simultaneously (block 1) or there is alternating switching between tasks (block 2). According to cognitive and attention research, dual tasks can be performed simultaneously when at least one of the tasks is automatic. It is very difficult to perform dual tasks, when both are non-automatic due to the limitations of WM (Skaugset et al., 2016). Driving is an automatic process for experienced drivers, particularly in straightforward conditions like highway driving. However, once the driving task becomes complex (Si ́eroff, 2001), for example, increased traffic density, drivers have to switch their attention to driving. In this case, dual tasks can only be performed when the driving task remains easy enough that the driver can continue to drive while performing the secondary task for a short time. In most instances, drivers rapidly switch their attention between the driving and secondary tasks to continue driving and performing the secondary task. Fig. 6 relates to task switching during conditional automated driving. In Fig. 6, the NDRT is similar to task A in Fig. 2, and the resumption of manual driving can be considered task B. The switch can be initiated by the subjective intention of the driver or at the request of the automation system. Most researchers have investigated the latter situation, as shown in Table 2. The takeover request launched by the vehicle system is like the stimulus that calls for a task switch in the task switching paradigm, while the switch back to an NDRT from manual driving may not involve an overt stimulus. Road safety concerns are more likely to occur during the transition from automated to manual driving.
