5.2. Effects of switch cost on driving performance
Researchers have found that a switch cost exists if participants are involved in the task switching paradigm; switching costs lead to a temporary impairment of performance on both tasks. Local switch cost refers to the delayed reaction time and decreased accuracy rate that is immediately seen when the task is switched (Strobach et al., 2012). Consequently, when drivers switch from performing a cognitive task to driving, their reaction time and driving stability can be affected. Indeed, as shown in Tables 1 and 2, most studies have found that compared to not engaging in any cognitive task, switching from a cognitive task to a driving task resulted in increased reaction time and decreased driving stability in both manual driving and conditional automated driving. In theory, cognitive task performance after the switch back from driving should also be affected. However, this remains a topic for future research. Local switch cost relates to the instant influence of task switching during driving, whereas global switch cost, or mixing cost, relates to the concept that the performance of each task (even a repeated task in mixed blocks) is not as good as when only one task is performed (Gamboz et al., 2009; Strobach et al., 2012). This concept predicts that driving performance will be impaired for a protracted period after task switching. This prediction is supported by evidence that driving performance is better in pure manual driving than after takeover in conditional automated driving (Lu et al., 2021; Radlmayr et al., 2014). Since switch cost impairs driving after task switching, the duration of the switch cost is a vital issue for road safety. While most studies have demonstrated that it takes 20–40 s to normalise driving control following a takeover request (Merat, Jamson, Lai, Daly, & Carsten, 2014), estimates of the duration vary widely. At one extreme, it has been reported that driver SDLP and steering angle recover to normal levels 5 s after drivers resume control following a takeover request (Naujoks et al., 2019). A 5 s recovery time is remarkably short and is probably due to a minimal residual cost with adequate preparation, as shown by task switching research implying that the residual cost may last for 5 s in predictable circumstances (Kimberg et al., 2000; Sohn et al., 2000). At the other extreme, it has been reported that novice drivers can still be impaired 5 min after the takeover request and the drivers are unaware that their driving performance has degraded (Zhang et al., 2023). In that study, drivers were instructed to fully engage in the NDRT and ignore the driving conditions until requested to takeover. This naturalistic scenario resembles what will occur in level 3 automation and indicates that high levels of focused attention can greatly increase the duration of the switching cost. Another factor influencing the duration of the switch cost is task difficulty of the takeover scenario. When different road conditions were used (in terms of accident ahead and traffic density) it was found that the easiest road condition allowed drivers to stabilize the vehicle in 12 s compared to 23 s and 29 s for the accident condition and accident with congestion condition, respectively (Kim et al., 2021). In addition to driving parameters, physiological measures can indicate the duration of physical recovery from driving task switching. Researchers measured heart rate and heart rate variability during and after driver takeover, and their findings indicated that heart rate parameters take 20–30 s to stabilize following a low-demand planned takeover and 60 s to plateau (Melnicuk et al., 2021).
5.3. Factors influencing task switching during driving
Factors known to influence task switching in cognitive psychology are expected to influence task switching in driving scenarios. Task switching knowledge can help us identify factors likely to adversely affect driving performance so that road safety can be improved.
5.3.1. Preparation effect
The primary factor that influences task switch performance is the preparation effect, which relates to the time needed to mentally prepare a response to an anticipated stimulus. If insufficient preparation time is available, the response to the stimulus will be delayed. Prolonging the time interval before stimulus onset can reduce switch costs (Kiesel et al., 2010). Therefore, time budget, or lead time is considered to directly contribute to driving switch cost. Research consistently highlights the impact of time budget on switch costs in driving scenarios. When switching from NDRTs to driving, those drivers allotted a longer lead time, ranging from 6 to 10 s, exhibit greater stability in their driving performance compared to those with a shorter time budget of less than 5 s (Chen, Lu, Lin, Zhai, & Tan, 2021; Du, Kim, et al., 2020; Du, Yang, & Zhou, 2020; Lin, Lyu, Zhang, & Ma, 2021). The longer time budget allows drivers to benefit from the preparation effect to switch back to driving more effectively. These findings highlight the importance of allowing enough time for task preparation in conditional automated driving scenarios, leading to a safer and more controlled driving experience.
