The quality of preparation time is another factor. To effectively reduce switch costs, switch-specific preparation time is important, rather than temporal preparation (Kiesel et al., 2010; Rogers & Monsell, 1995). A long lead time is not advantageous if attention is being directed to another task during that time. By applying this point of view to task switching in automated driving, information about upcoming takeover tasks should be introduced to drivers before the occurrence of switching to allow for effective preparation. For example, when different levels of explanations were given regarding upcoming takeover scenarios, those participants with more detailed explanations displayed a significantly higher rate of successful interventions (Zhou et al., 2021). Therefore, in driving task switching, clear and targeted instructions help drivers to switch more effectively.
5.3.2. Interferences in task switching
As mentioned earlier, interferences in task switching come from proactive task interference, stimulus-based interference, and response-based interference. The following subsections discuss the interferences occurring in driving task switching.
5.3.2.1. Proactive task interference. Interference from proactive tasks comes from switch-cost asymmetries (Kiesel et al., 2010). In task switching, one unconsciously inhibits the skilled task in order to perform the unfamiliar task. In automated driving the type of NDRT can affect driving performance. After performing a NDRT that requires their full attention, the driver may demonstrate an inability to properly control the vehicle after switching. This type of impairment has been demonstrated using different levels of n-back tasks. As the n level increased, drivers’ reaction time and driving stability after the takeover worsened (Du, Kim, et al., 2020; Du, Yang, & Zhou, 2020; Melnicuk, Thompson, Jennings, & Birrell, 2021; Meteier et al., 2022).
5.3.2.2. Stimulus-based interference. In task switching, stimuli are considered to trigger retrieval of the task and provoke a larger switch time, with univalent stimuli involving a smaller switch cost than bivalent stimuli (Waszak et al., 2003). In automated driving the stimuli are neither univalent nor bivalent, because a stimulus cues the switch from an NDRT yet the switch from driving to an NDRT is generally left up to the discretion of the driver. Nonetheless, the type of stimulus may influence the speed of switching from NDRT to driving. A study of various warnings, including A-pillar LED light, earcon, speech messages, and haptic seat feedback (Hong & Yang, 2022) found that while strong warnings such as the haptic seat and the A-pillar LED light marginally reduced RT, they concurrently elevated stress levels and compromised overall driving stability. While an enhanced RT is beneficial, the potential increase in stress levels and reduced stability denotes the need for a balanced approach when designing human-vehicle interaction systems. Striking this balance becomes vital in ensuring efficient task performance and the well-being and safety of the driver.
5.3.2.3. Response-based interference. An aspect of response-based interference is the response-repetition effect. This effect relates to the fact that repetition of a response improves performance of the repeated task, but at the cost of the switching task (Kiesel et al., 2010). Thus, repetitive responding to one task adversely influences the performance of the other task. This effect is similar to engagement in NDRTs during automated driving, because repetition of the NDRT task equates to spending a longer time engaging in this task. Indeed, two studies have demonstrated that engaging in an NDRT for 20 min worsens takeover performance and vigilance level to a greater extent than engaging in the same NDRT for 5 min (Feldhütter, Gold, Schneider, & Bengler, 2017; Zhang, Fard, Xu, Davy, & Robinson, 2023).
5.3.3. Other factors
Apart from factors correlated with task switching, other factors are known to influence the cognitive load either by influencing the driver or the driving environment. These factors are considered below.
5.3.3.1. Driver-related factors. Driver Age. Although there are substantial individual differences in WM capacity (Kotyusov et al., 2023; Robison & Unsworth, 2017), there is a trend towards slower RTs and poorer WM as we grow older (Gajewski et al., 2018). Studies have reported poorer secondary task performance and worse driving performance among elder people when compared to
younger people (O ̈ ztürk et al., 2023; Peng et al., 2022; Uci ́nska et al., 2021). While tasks involved the same cognitive load, the elder group may have experienced a higher cognitive workload, resulting in a deterioration in performance. Brain imaging studies have shown that increased age is associated with a strong increase in global switch costs whereas increases in local switch costs are more modest (Eich et al., 2023). This difference suggests that in conditional automated driving, elders may switch successfully from NDRTs to driving, yet their driving performance after switching may take longer to recover to control levels than younger people. Driver Experience. Drivers acquire attentional skills and decision-making skills through experience (Drummond, 1989). Through practice, driving skills can be stored in LTM, which enables these skills to be employed automatically. Consequently, inexperienced drivers utilise more WM when driving and are more prone to cognitive overload. As a result, novice drivers experience a higher accident rate. For instance, in the State of Victoria, Australia, probationary drivers have a significantly higher risk of casualty crashes in both daytime and late night (VicRoads, 2023b). Similar results are observed for level 3 automated driving. Novice drivers have a similar takeover response time to experienced drivers, yet they have poorer takeover stability and weak adaptability in terms of lateral control in different urgency scenarios (Chen et al., 2021). In support of this finding, researchers reported that following a takeover response, inexperienced drivers spent significantly more time out of the lane and exhibited more erratic lateral control of the vehicle. Interestingly, years of driving experience were a less significant factor than the number of kilometres driven, with a maximum benefit gained from 10,000 km of driving per year and 100,000 km of driving in total (Zhang et al., 2023). These findings suggest that considerable driving experience is required to fully encode driving skills into LTM.
