5.3.3.2. Environmental factors. Visibility. Visibility on the road, affected by weather and the time of day, significantly impacts driver cognitive load. Reduced visibility, especially during nighttime or in fog, demands heightened cognitive efforts for situational awareness. Research indicates that participants exhibit poorer driving stability in low-visibility conditions, underscoring the chal
lenges drivers face during nighttime travel (  ̈Oztürk et al., 2023). Road conditions. Road conditions play a pivotal role in shaping driver task switching performance, particularly regarding traffic density. Road conditions also include changes in road curvature, road configuration, changes in road surface conditions and so on. Recent research has focussed on traffic density. Heavy traffic requires more situational awareness and a greater number of decisions, which significantly increases the cognitive load for drivers, impacting their ability to efficiently switch between tasks (Du, Kim, et al., 2020; Du, Yang, & Zhou, 2020).
6. Discussion
6.1. Should distracted tasks be permitted in level 3 automated driving?
The present review has shown that the process of switching attention from one task to another may appear to be quick from an observer’s standpoint, yet a complete switch of attention, as indicated by the normalisation of objective performance measures, can require between 20 s and 5 min. In the context of level 3 automated driving, it means that a driver will drive worse than normally (in some cases dangerously out-of-lane) for up to 5 min after resuming control of their vehicle following a takeover request. For task switching and multitasking in manual driving, extensive research and discussions have highlighted the deterioration of driving performance and the increased rate of road accidents caused by distracting tasks (Llerena et al., 2015; Overton et al., 2015). Society has acknowledged the dangers of distracted driving, prompting legislation in most countries that limits the types of secondary tasks drivers can engage in. For example, in the State of Victoria, Australia, learners and probationary drivers are prohibited from using any mobile device, including mobile phones, while driving. In contrast, fully licensed drivers are permitted to make or receive phone calls and use navigation systems, provided they are not handheld (VicRoads, 2023a). This difference is due to the recognition that young drivers, who are often less experienced, are over-represented in serious road crashes. Also, the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA) issued driver distraction guidelines for in-vehicle electronic devices (NHTSA, 2012). This document advises against in-vehicle electronic systems that allow drivers to perform some activities when the vehicle is moving, especially visual-manual demanded activities. However, there appears to be a lack of discussion regarding restrictions on NDRTs in the forthcoming era of conditional automated driving. It needs to be acknowledged that most current studies of conditional automated driving rely on data obtained in driving simulators, primarily due to regulatory constraints and safety concerns. How these findings will translate to real-world driving scenarios requires further investigation. Having said that, the driving simulator data that are currently available consistently demonstrate that task switching impairs driving performance, and there is no reason to believe that this will change in the real world. The SAE standard for on-road motor automation systems (SAE, 2021) describes drivers in level 3 as dynamic driving task (DDT) fallback-ready users. This standard denotes that as DDT fallback-ready users in level 3 automation, drivers do not need to monitor the vehicle, yet they are expected to be prepared to either resume DDT performance or ensure minimal risk conditions. This means that drivers are responsible for actively switching their attention from other tasks to driving. The data in the present review show that drivers consistently perform worse when engaged in task switching than when they are not. This decline in performance can be mitigated to some extent but not eliminated. Additionally, different types of NDRTs have varying levels of negative effects on driver takeover performance. Hence, the concern persists regarding whether all NDRTs are safe enough for all drivers, particularly for inexperienced ones. In this context, evaluating the cognitive load of naturalistic demands becomes crucial. While cognitive psychology research has classified the levels of cognitive demand for daily tasks (Romero-Ayuso et al., 2021), the assessment of in-vehicle activities remains a topic of discussion. In summary, these concerns raise questions for vehicle manufacturers, governments, and road users alike: What type of humanvehicle interaction design can effectively mitigate the risks associated with driver task switching, particularly in higher levels of automation? Are novice drivers or elderly drivers capable of effectively switching their attention back to driving? What types of NDRTs fall within a safe range for use on the road by drivers?
6.2. Suggestions to improve road safety with higher-level automation
The present authors recommend that before higher levels of vehicle automation are permitted on suburban roads, protections ought to be put in place to reduce the safety concerns raised by the inevitable task switching during driving. The following paragraphs outline potential ways to reduce the risks associated with driver task switching. For vehicle Automated Driving Systems (ADS) design. Driver preparation can reduce switch costs, and a longer lead time free of cognitive tasks can improve driver takeover stability. With current research that is mostly based on young drivers, once the driver ceases engaging in an NDRT, they require 6 s to refocus their attention on the road conditions before taking control of the vehicle. Ideally, ADS should incorporate driver preparation time prior to the takeover. This suggestion has been addressed by the United Nations Economic Commission for Europe (UNECE) in UN Relation No. 157. This regulation requires a minimum of 10 s to be provided to transition to manual driving for an automated lane-keeping system (UNECE, 2021). Furthermore, to assist takeover in level 3 automation, drivers will need to be repeatedly exposed to takeover requests under safe conditions, until they develop automatic responses to takeover requests that are stored in their LTM. Such training sessions should be a
