feature of the ADS. Since there is individual variability in the speed and accuracy of takeover responses, the ADS should be able to build a predictive profile of the driver to anticipate the time required by the driver to return to a safe level of driving performance after a takeover. It follows from this that the ADS will need to be able to recognise when a different driver is behind the wheel. Finally, the ADS will require backup strategies that can be quickly implemented in the case of unsuccessful takeovers. For regulations and laws. Regulatory authorities will need to prescribe which types and durations of NDRTs are acceptable for drivers to engage in during level 3 conditional automated driving. The permissibility of probationary licensed drivers using level 3 automation will require resolution before conditional automated driving is legally implemented. Authorities ought to consider what driving conditions are permissible for drivers to engage in NDRTs. Automated driving on arterial roads during the daytime in good weather may be acceptable, but do local roads at night, in rain, carry too much risk? For drivers. Drivers should first acquire manual driving abilities before using level 3 conditional automated driving functions. It is recommended that drivers should first gain extensive manual driving experience to reduce the cognitive load of basic driving tasks. Additionally, drivers should refer to instructions to familiarize themselves with vehicle controls before using level 3 automation. Drivers need to be educated about task switching and the associated decline in driving performance, particularly as drivers report no awareness that their driving performance has declined after a takeover (Zhang et al., 2023). Another suggestion is that drivers will need to use the manual controls regularly, as there is a risk of diminishing automaticity due to infrequency of use. This is happening in the aviation industry where automation reduces the risk of human errors most of the time as the shown in the safety record, but automation has the potential to gradually diminish cognitive skills, which only becomes apparent when the pilot is required to respond exceptional circumstances (Oliver et al., 2017). For further research. As driving task switching research is mostly based on driving simulators, on-road tests will be required to validate the results from simulation studies. In addition:
I. Further studies are needed to identify which NDRTs are compatible with rapid and accurate takeovers, and which NDRTs most greatly compromise driving performance after a takeover. The duration of the NDRT also needs to be examined, as most studies to date have examined short durations of a few minutes, whereas the activity of watching a movie could potentially last more than an hour in real life. Engagement in these NDRTs should involve a maximal level of focussed attention since this is expected in real-life scenarios. II. The available data are largely based on healthy young university students, making it likely that these data are not representative of most drivers. Future studies of task switching in conditional automated driving should compare driver factors such as driving experience, driver age, driver gender or the influence of common health disorders (e.g. obstructive sleep apnoea, insomnia, obesity, subclinical depression, prescription medications). Notably, the population spread of performance after takeover needs to be understood, as the population mean is an unreliable indicator. Road accidents are more likely to be caused by those individuals who are outliers (e.g. 2 standard deviations above the mean) in terms of the duration required to return to normal driving performance after a takeover. III. While intervention and self-interruption have been found to reduce takeover RT, repetitive and unnecessary task switching can increase the cognitive load with minimal benefits (Y. Wu et al., 2019). From a road safety perspective, the optimal frequency of interruption from NDRTs remains to be determined. Insights into this could be gained from research on Driver Monitoring Systems (Braunagel et al., 2017; Guettas et al., 2020; Yu et al., 2021). DMS have been increasingly studied in recent years to detect and classify driver states—such as inattention, posture changes, fatigue, stress, and emotion—using physiological, behavioural, and vehicle-based approaches (Guettas et al., 2020; Hecht et al., 2019). By continuously monitoring driver states, DMS can help trigger timely interruptions when drivers are at risk of losing control, ensuring they successfully switch back to the driving task.
6.3. Limitations
The primary limitation of the present review is that it considers standardised tasks which would not occur during normal driving, instead of reviewing the effects of the types of distractions that would normally occur during driving. As we have noted, standardised tasks are widely used by researchers because they reliably and completely distract attention and can be readily replicated between studies. In contrast, real world distractions are inherently variable in duration and in the amount of attention they require, which makes them difficult to quantify in a laboratory setting. Nonetheless, there is a need for more research that uses distraction paradigms that are close to real-life situations. Another limitation of the scope of the present review is that, due to space limitations, we only considered studies with young and healthy participants. Research has been published on task switching in drivers with cognitive impairment due to factors such as degenerative disease (Ahmadnezhad et al., 2022; Ahmadnezhad et al., 2023), alcohol and drug intoxication (Wiedemann et al., 2018), or medication use, and these studies consistently show that these factors greatly lengthen the time taken to switch attention.
7. Conclusion
This review explained attention and cognitive processes involved in driving and applied task switching concepts to manual driving and level 3 conditional automated driving. Data consistently show that cognitive overload and switch costs lead to a significant delay in resuming control of the vehicle and driving safely. In manual driving, when drivers undertake distracting tasks, their performance for both driving and the cognitive task can be impaired. In conditional automated driving, switching between NDRTs and driving not
